% Origin: Kowalski, *Computational Logic and Human Thinking*,
%         Chapter 7 "Logic", section 7.8, Example 13
%         ("Explicit-goal conditional for equation solving").
% Last verification: no open issues (no errors/warnings; all tests pass).
%
% This is an ABSTRACT, illustrative goal-conditional. The book's rule reads:
% "An equation with a single variable X is solved if all occurrences of X are
% combined into a single occurrence and the single occurrence of X is isolated."
% Only this top-level goal is a logic-programming conditional; the lower-level
% rewriting steps that actually combine and isolate occurrences are production
% rules, NOT Logical English. We therefore render the two sub-goals as
% predicates whose truth is supplied by scenarios, and faithfully encode just
% the top-level conditional.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
*an equation* is solved.
the occurrences of the variable in *an equation* are combined.
the single occurrence of the variable in *an equation* is isolated.

the knowledge base equation solving includes:

% The book's top-level goal-conditional, rendered declaratively.
an equation is solved
    if the occurrences of the variable in the equation are combined
    and the single occurrence of the variable in the equation is isolated.

scenario solved is:
    the occurrences of the variable in eq1 are combined.
    the single occurrence of the variable in eq1 is isolated.
    which solved expects answers ["eq1 is solved"].

scenario partial is:
    the occurrences of the variable in eq2 are combined.
    which solved expects answers [].

query which solved is:
    which equation is solved.
